<GuideLayout title="Intermediate 1.1 · Wintertodt Foundation" current="intermediate">
  <div class="page-grid">
    <aside class="sidebar"><IntermediateNav current="1-1" /></aside>
    <article class="content">
      <header class="chapter-header">
        <div class="chapter-kicker">Intermediate · Chapter 1.1</div>
        <h1 class="chapter-title">Build the early account, then use Wintertodt as a resource engine.</h1>
        <p class="chapter-summary">This section establishes the first quest, cash, travel and Construction foundation before the long Firemaking block. Finish each quest, bank when useful, and keep only future pickups that save a meaningful return trip; nothing in this subsection requires another guide open beside it.</p>
      </header>

      <ChapterGoals
        summary="By the end of 1.1 you should have the important early quest foundation, a player-owned house, four guaranteed warm items, 35+ Woodcutting and roughly 89 Firemaking. The Intermediate route stops around 89, but taking Wintertodt all the way to 99 Firemaking is a valid optional choice if you want to front-load the skill and rewards."
        stats={["35+ Woodcutting before Wintertodt", "50 Firemaking before entering Wintertodt", "About 89 Firemaking on the recommended route", "99 Firemaking optional if you want to finish the skill now", "Early Thieving/Fletching/Construction gained naturally"]}
        unlocks={["Ghostspeak amulet banked for later quests", "Ardougne and Gnome-area starter quest foundation", "Player-owned house before Wintertodt repairs", "Four easy warm items", "Wintertodt Reward Cart stocked for later Chapter 1 supplies"]}
      />

      <RouteStep id="i11-start" number="1" title="Build the Lumbridge/Draynor starter bank and finish the first quests">
        <p>Keep a <strong>knife, hammer, chisel, axe, pickaxe, tinderbox, spade, shears, a pot and at least 2 buckets</strong>. Count Tutorial Island tools first. Buy missing general tools at <strong>Lumbridge General Store</strong>, an axe from <strong>Bob</strong>, and take a free bronze pickaxe from the top of either Lumbridge Castle gate tower if needed. For a small coin shortfall, pickpocket nearby men/women; cook chickens at the Lumbridge farms for food when needed. Burn normal logs to <strong>15 Firemaking</strong> and bank <strong>5 ashes</strong>. You do <strong>not</strong> need to build a large stack of normal logs here; the default route will use the logs you cut for Firemaking. If you prefer to bank some logs and burn them later, that is perfectly fine.</p>
        <p>Complete these quests one at a time:</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>X Marks the Spot</strong></li>
          <li><strong>The Restless Ghost</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Misthalin Mystery</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Rune Mysteries</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Sheep Shearer</strong></li>
        </ol>
        <ExpandableNote title="The first five quests: what you supply and what the quest supplies" label="Quest preparation">
          <ul>
            <li><strong>X Marks the Spot:</strong> bring your <strong>spade</strong>; buy a replacement at Lumbridge General Store if needed. Veos and the dig sites supply the treasure scrolls, orb and casket.</li>
            <li><strong>The Restless Ghost:</strong> no outside quest items. Father Urhney in the <strong>south-west Lumbridge Swamp hut</strong> gives the ghostspeak amulet; return to him if lost. The skull is in the Wizards' Tower basement. Take food for the trip, but you can leave the skeleton instead of fighting it.</li>
            <li><strong>Misthalin Mystery:</strong> no outside items are required. The island supplies the bucket, knife, tinderbox, keys and other puzzle objects; search its rooms/barrels during the quest. Your reusable knife/tinderbox are convenient if already carried.</li>
            <li><strong>Rune Mysteries:</strong> no outside items. The Duke supplies the air talisman, Sedridor supplies the research package and Aubury supplies the research notes. Ask the relevant NPC again for a lost delivery item.</li>
            <li><strong>Sheep Shearer:</strong> bring <strong>20 balls of wool</strong>. The Ardougne wool purchase happens later: shear twenty sheep north of Lumbridge now, then spin the raw wool at the spinning wheel upstairs in Lumbridge Castle. Unspun wool cannot be handed in. Refill missing shears at the general store.</li>
          </ul>
        </ExpandableNote>
        <p>After The Restless Ghost, keep the <strong>ghostspeak amulet</strong> in your bank. Do not discard it; Nature Spirit, Fairytale I and other later quests reuse it.</p>
        <div class="later-note"><strong>Lamps:</strong> use generic early XP lamps on <strong>Herblore</strong> unless a later step explicitly says otherwise. Druidic Ritual is in 1.2. The X Marks the Spot and later Client of Kourend lamps <strong>cannot be banked</strong>; destroy those reclaimable lamps to free inventory space, then reclaim them from Veos after Druidic Ritual. Do not apply that destroy instruction to unrelated lamps.</div>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i11-ardy-quests" number="2" title="Clear the first Ardougne quest block, then combine the Woodcutting and Firemaking grind">
        <p>Complete <strong>Clock Tower</strong>, <strong>Monk's Friend</strong> and <strong>Hazeel Cult</strong> as three separate quests. After each quest, bank if the next inventory would be cleaner.</p>
        <p><strong>First trip to Ardougne:</strong> take the ordinary Port Sarim ship to Karamja for <strong>30 coins</strong>, walk west to Brimhaven, then pay Captain Barnaby <strong>30 coins</strong> to sail to Ardougne. Bring food for the walk; these ordinary boats do not need a teleport unlock.</p>
        <ExpandableNote title="Water in two different containers, and one ordinary log" label="Quest preparation">
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Clock Tower:</strong> bring <strong>1 bucket of water</strong> for the hot black cog. Fill a bucket at the well by Cedric, east of the tower; a bucket also spawns there. The cogs and rat poison are found in the tower dungeon. Ice gloves are an optional substitute for the water bucket.</li>
            <li><strong>Monk's Friend:</strong> bring <strong>1 ordinary log + 1 jug of water</strong>. Chop a normal tree with your axe; buy a jug at a general store and fill it at a fountain/sink. Cedric needs a jug, so the Clock Tower bucket is the wrong container. The child's blanket is recovered in the quest's cave.</li>
            <li><strong>Hazeel Cult:</strong> no outside quest item purchases. The cult branch obtains poison from Clivet and the key/scroll from the Carnillean house. If you choose the Ceril branch, bring your starter weapon and food for the quest fight; the missing armour is recovered locally.</li>
          </ul>
        </ExpandableNote>
<TrainingOptions id="i11-wc-fm-training" informational={true} skill="Woodcutting + Firemaking" target="35 Woodcutting / 50 Firemaking before Wintertodt" why="35 Woodcutting is the useful early Woodcutting breakpoint, while 50 Firemaking is the hard requirement to enter Wintertodt. There is no benefit to treating these as two unrelated grinds: the logs you cut for Woodcutting are also your Firemaking XP." recommended="Cut normal trees until 15 Woodcutting, then move to oaks. Burn the logs you cut as you go. Reach 35 Woodcutting, then continue cutting/burning as needed until 50 Firemaking. If you would rather return to questing once you hit 35 Woodcutting, that is fine too—just finish 50 Firemaking before Step 7 when you actually enter Wintertodt." relaxed="Use a Forestry world and cut in a comfortable populated spot. You can bank inventories and burn them later instead of alternating constantly; banking is an option for convenience, not a route requirement." resource="If it is convenient, keep some spare oak logs banked for later Fletching/quest use, but do not force a large log stockpile here. Wintertodt and later Woodcutting will provide more logs." fast="2-tick methods can save time, but they are never required or assumed by this guide." />
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i11-ardy-supplies" number="3" title="Do the Ardougne supply run once, then finish the local quests">
        <p><strong>Cash and food first:</strong> use the completed local quests' coins for the first purchases. If short, steal cakes in East Ardougne from <strong>5 Thieving</strong> and silk from <strong>20 Thieving</strong>; pickpocket men/women to 5 if necessary, then use cakes to reach 20. Bank the food and silk, let the silk merchant's stolen-goods refusal expire while questing, then negotiate a sale for <strong>60 coins per silk</strong>. Repeat only until the actual shop bill is covered. Keep <strong>11 silk</strong> out of those sales.</p>
        <p>At <strong>Aemad's Adventuring Supplies in East Ardougne</strong>, count existing supplies, then buy and bank the shortfall to:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>3 ropes</strong></li>
          <li><strong>1 water-filled vial pack</strong></li>
          <li><strong>30 balls of wool</strong></li>
          <li><strong>7 papyrus</strong></li>
        </ul>
        <p>At the <strong>Silver Merchant</strong>, reserve up to <strong>5 silver bars</strong> for separate quest uses, after counting banked bars or already-owned finished items: <strong>Shadow of the Storm's demonic sigil</strong>, <strong>Nature Spirit's sickle</strong>, <strong>Creature of Fenkenstrain's conductor</strong>, <strong>Animal Magnetism's holy symbol if needed</strong>, and <strong>Desert Treasure's hand-in</strong>. The merchant stocks only one bar at a time, so use restocks or other worlds for the shortfall; do not presume five are on one shelf. Keep this quest reserve separate from any optional silver jewellery, and recheck it at each consumer below. Across this block, steal and bank at least <strong>100 cakes</strong>, <strong>14 bread</strong> and <strong>84 silk</strong>. The cakes are the starting Wintertodt food bank; replace any eaten during questing before that activity. Sell silk through the cash loop above while retaining the <strong>11-silk quest reserve</strong>. If stock is sold out, wait for a restock or use another world; an empty shelf is not a banked item.</p>
        <p>Now complete <strong>Sheep Herder</strong>, <strong>Sea Slug</strong> and <strong>Tribal Totem</strong> one at a time. During Sheep Herder, bring a <strong>spade</strong> and dig up the <strong>clue hunter gloves</strong> and <strong>clue hunter boots</strong> north of Ardougne near the Fishing Guild. These are two of the four Wintertodt warm items.</p>
        <ExpandableNote title="Buy Sea Slug's swamp paste before sailing to the platform" label="Quest preparation">
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Sheep Herder:</strong> bring <strong>100 coins</strong> and buy the protective outfit from Doctor Orbon after starting with Councillor Halgrive. Halgrive supplies the sheep feed, and the cattle prod is inside Farmer Brumty's enclosure. Your spade is for the nearby clue hunter clothing pickup, not a separate quest purchase.</li>
            <li><strong>Sea Slug:</strong> check <strong>30 Firemaking</strong> and buy <strong>1 finished swamp paste from Khazard General Store at Port Khazard</strong> before talking to Holgart for the boat repair. Raw swamp paste or swamp tar is the wrong form. If sold out, wait/restock or use another world. Bailey supplies the torch, and the platform supplies the broken glass/damp sticks used to light it. No fishing rod is required.</li>
            <li><strong>Tribal Totem:</strong> check <strong>21 Thieving</strong>; steal a little more silk if needed. Bring <strong>90 coins</strong> for three ordinary Ardougne–Brimhaven boat crossings, with extra only if you choose additional banking trips. Cromperty's delivery depot supplies the address label used during the quest; no totem or special label is bought beforehand.</li>
          </ul>
        </ExpandableNote>
        <div class="later-note"><strong>Brimhaven pickup:</strong> while doing Tribal Totem, buy and bank <strong>7 soda ash, 7 buckets of sand, 1 cake tin and 1 glassblowing pipe</strong> from the charter shop. Reserve <strong>one sand + one soda ash</strong> for Observatory Quest's molten glass in 1.2. Refill missing charter stock by waiting or changing worlds. Sea Slug's paste was bought before its own step above.</div>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i11-starter-cleanup" number="4" title="Finish the remaining early quest foundation before Wintertodt">
        <p>Complete the following as standalone quests. Quest Helper can handle dialogue and puzzle details; the important part is that none are left half-finished:</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Cook's Assistant</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Pirate's Treasure</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Gertrude's Cat</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Romeo & Juliet</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Daddy's Home</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Witch's Potion</strong></li>
          <li><strong>The Knight's Sword</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Tower of Life</strong></li>
        </ol>
        <ExpandableNote title="Eight early quests: food forms, Construction materials and the iron-bar recovery" label="Quest preparation">
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Cook's Assistant:</strong> <strong>1 egg, 1 pot of flour and 1 bucket of milk</strong>. Take an egg from a Lumbridge chicken farm, milk a dairy cow with an empty bucket, and put wheat through the windmill north-west of Lumbridge; collect the flour in a pot. Buy missing pot/bucket at the general store.</li>
            <li><strong>Pirate's Treasure:</strong> bring <strong>60 coins and a spade</strong>. Sail from Port Sarim to Karamja, buy the rum from Zambo's pub, and put it with <strong>10 bananas</strong> from the nearby plantation in Luthas's crate. Use the quest's shipping method so the rum survives the journey. Luthas pays for the return fare. A <strong>white apron</strong> spawns in Gerrant's Port Sarim fishing shop for the Wydin storeroom step; take one, then obtain the chest key/message during the quest.</li>
            <li><strong>Gertrude's Cat:</strong> <strong>100 coins, 1 bucket of milk and 1 raw sardine</strong>. Milk another Lumbridge cow and buy a raw sardine at <strong>Lovecraft's Tackle in Witchaven</strong>; wait for stock or use another world if needed. Pick doogle leaves just south of Gertrude's house and use them on the raw sardine. A cooked sardine will not work. The kitten is found in the lumberyard crates.</li>
            <li><strong>Romeo &amp; Juliet:</strong> pick <strong>one bunch of cadava berries</strong> from the bushes south-east of Varrock. Juliet supplies her letter, and the Apothecary makes the potion during the quest.</li>
            <li><strong>Daddy's Home:</strong> <strong>10 normal planks, 5 bolts of cloth, a hammer, a saw and at least 14 successful nails</strong>. Bring about <strong>5,000 coins</strong> and ten normal logs to the <strong>Varrock sawmill</strong>: convert the logs at 100 coins each, buy the five cloth at 650 coins each, buy a saw, and take about <strong>100 steel nails</strong> so bent nails do not halt the job. Use existing supplies first; if more nails bend, buy another small batch. The three waxwood logs are in Yarlo's crates, and the sawmill converts those quest logs for free. Marlo gives a <strong>free house if you do not own one</strong>, plus supplies including 25 normal planks and 50 mithril nails. Keep those rewards for the bridge and Observatory below.</li>
            <li><strong>Witch's Potion:</strong> <strong>1 onion, 1 burnt meat and 1 eye of newt</strong>. Pick the onion north of Rimmington and buy the eye from Betty in Port Sarim. Cook beef from a Lumbridge cow, then use the cooked meat on the range again to burn it deliberately. Start the quest before killing a small Rimmington rat for its quest-only rat's tail.</li>
            <li><strong>The Knight's Sword:</strong> <strong>1 whole redberry pie, 2 iron bars and a pickaxe</strong>, with <strong>10 Mining</strong> for the quest's blurite rock. Mine copper/tin at Rimmington to reach the live Mining gate if needed. Make the pie at <strong>10 Cooking</strong>: mix flour and water into pastry dough, put it in a pie dish from the Varrock Palace kitchen, add redberries picked south-east of Varrock, and cook it on a range. Cook's Assistant and Gertrude's Cat cover 10 Cooking if completed; repeat the recipe if it burns, and do not eat a slice. For the iron bars, check the bank first. An empty bank can be filled by mining copper/tin, smelting bronze at a furnace and smithing bronze items with a hammer at the anvils by Varrock west bank until <strong>15 Smithing</strong>; reach <strong>15 Mining</strong>, mine iron at Rimmington, then smelt until two bars succeed. Ordinary iron smelting can fail, so mine more ore as needed. Alternatively, low-level dwarves around Ice Mountain/the Dwarven Mine drop iron bars; bring food and a weapon and stop once you have two, without assuming a fixed kill count. Mine the blurite in the quest's ice dungeon south of Port Sarim and obtain the portrait in Falador Castle during the quest.</li>
            <li><strong>Tower of Life:</strong> <strong>1 beer, a hammer and saw</strong>, plus <strong>actual 10 Construction</strong>. Daddy's Home alone ends at level 8. If still below 10, enter building mode at your Rimmington house and build/remove crude wooden chairs in the parlour until the live level reaches 10. From Daddy's Home's level 8, <strong>four chairs use 8 reward planks and at least 8 nails</strong>; bring spare reward nails for bends. Keep <strong>3 other normal planks</strong> for Observatory Quest. Buy the beer at Ardougne's Flying Horse Inn. The workers and tower crates supply the builder outfit and all repair parts. The chicken/swordfish used for optional diary work are not quest requirements.</li>
          </ul>
          <p><strong>Replace food actually used</strong> from Ardougne's cake stalls before the next quest. These are separate inventories, so bank between them. Keep the reusable saw/hammer/pickaxe and remaining nails afterwards; buy replacements from their named suppliers only when the live bank is short.</p>
        </ExpandableNote>
        <p>Confirm that you <strong>claimed the free player-owned house from Marlo</strong> after Daddy's Home before the Wintertodt grind. Owning a house matters because repairing broken braziers at Wintertodt can award Construction XP.</p>
        <div class="later-note"><strong>Cat background option:</strong> Gertrude gives you a kitten. If it fits the activity, let it follow during long skilling/quest sessions; feed and stroke it when it asks. It takes about <strong>three hours of following</strong> to mature. This is not a Chapter 1 checkbox or a guaranteed death-rune bank. A follower can interfere with other activities, so bank it when inconvenient. After Biohazard in 1.4, the Ardougne Easy diary makes grown-cat exchanges much more valuable; keep a suitable grown cat for RFD Evil Dave before trading extras.</div>
        <div class="optional-efficiency"><strong>Deferred on purpose:</strong> quests that would otherwise be started here only to save a future walk—such as The Corsair Curse, A Porcine of Interest, Black Knights' Fortress and Demon Slayer—are deliberately left untouched for now. They will be completed later when the route can finish them cleanly.</div>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i11-kourend" number="5" title="Complete Client of Kourend before the long skilling break">
        <p>Buy <strong>1 feather from Gerrant in Port Sarim</strong> or kill a chicken for one, then take <strong>Veos's ship from Port Sarim to Port Piscarilius</strong> and complete <strong>Client of Kourend</strong> in one go. Veos supplies the enchanted scroll and later orb; use the feather to make the quill, and return to him for lost quest objects. Walk between the five general stores and the Dark Altar, or pay the optional minecart fares from spare coins. This establishes the Kourend travel/context used later in Chapter 1 without carrying an unfinished quest through Wintertodt.</p>
        <p>The two reward lamps cannot be banked. If saving them for Herblore, destroy them and <strong>reclaim them from Veos after Druidic Ritual in 1.2</strong>. Keep Kharedst's memoirs banked for its later page unlocks.</p>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i11-warm" number="6" title="Finish the four-piece Wintertodt clothing set">
        <p>You already have the clue hunter gloves and boots. Travel to <strong>Gilbert, southeast of Barbarian Village</strong>, and claim the free <strong>rainbow jumper</strong> and <strong>rainbow scarf</strong>. Equip these four items at Wintertodt:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Clue hunter gloves</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Clue hunter boots</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Rainbow jumper</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Rainbow scarf</strong></li>
        </ul>
        <div class="callout"><strong>Why exactly four?</strong> The current Wintertodt system gives its maximum warm-clothing benefit at four warm items. A fifth warm item does not add another tier of benefit.</div>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i11-wt-prep" number="7" title="Enter Wintertodt with one repeatable inventory">
        <div class="callout"><strong>Hard check before entering:</strong> Wintertodt requires <strong>50 Firemaking</strong>. If you stopped the combined Woodcutting/Firemaking block at 35 Woodcutting without reaching 50 Firemaking, finish burning logs now.</div>
        <p>Equip the <strong>four warm items</strong> from Step 6. Bring:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Your best axe</li>
          <li><strong>Tinderbox</strong> or bruma torch</li>
          <li><strong>Knife</strong> if you want to fletch bruma roots</li>
          <li><strong>Hammer</strong> for brazier repairs and Construction XP</li>
          <li>About <strong>6 cakes</strong> for a normal mass-world trip; adjust after a few games if you consistently use fewer or more</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Modern Wintertodt uses a <strong>Warmth meter</strong>, not the old direct Hitpoints-damage model. Low Hitpoints is no longer the account-building advantage older guides describe. Four warm items improve Warmth management, and food restores Warmth as well as Hitpoints.</p>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i11-wt-loop" number="8" title="Use this simple Wintertodt loop">
        <ol>
          <li>At the start of a round, light a brazier if it is unlit.</li>
          <li>Chop <strong>bruma roots</strong>.</li>
          <li>Optional: fletch roots into <strong>bruma kindling</strong> for more points and Fletching XP.</li>
          <li>Feed roots/kindling into a lit brazier.</li>
          <li>If the brazier breaks, repair it with your hammer; if it goes out, relight it.</li>
          <li>Eat when your Warmth gets uncomfortable; do not let Warmth reach 0%.</li>
          <li>Earn at least <strong>500 points</strong> before the round ends so you receive rewards.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Rewards accumulate in the <strong>Reward Cart</strong> outside the camp bank. There is no need to open a supply crate after every game.</p>
        <div class="later-note"><strong>Passive Woodcutting matters later:</strong> chopping bruma roots awards Woodcutting XP scaled to your current level. Starting Wintertodt around 35 Woodcutting and staying to the recommended ~89 Firemaking will usually move Woodcutting substantially upward, often near the 60 Woodcutting Guild breakpoint. Do not force 60 here if you finish lower; the Zeah block in 1.3 and the dedicated 1.4 Woodcutting break both include a fallback for buying the rune axe once 60 is actually reached.</div>
      </RouteStep>

      <TrainingOptions id="i11-wintertodt-training" skill="Firemaking" target="about 89 recommended; 99 optional" why="Around 89 Firemaking preserves the Chapter 1 supply/cash foundation without requiring you to finish a skill that has few important unlocks for this route. However, if you like Wintertodt or simply want Firemaking permanently finished, continuing all the way to 99 here is completely valid and gives you more rolls at the Reward Cart. The rest of this guide does not require 99." recommended="Use standard Wintertodt mass worlds and stop around 89 Firemaking. Fletch when you want extra points/Fletching XP; otherwise keep the loop simple." relaxed="Stay on one Wintertodt world and repeat normal games. You can stop around 89, or keep going toward 99 in as many sessions as you like if you prefer to finish the skill now." resource="Going to 99 means substantially more Wintertodt rewards and can leave you with a stronger early bank, but do it because you want the extra levels/rewards—not because Chapter 2 expects it." fast="Hopping into partially completed games can improve XP and rewards per hour, but it is optional and not assumed." />

      <RouteStep id="i11-finish" number="9" title="Leave Wintertodt with the resources the next sections will use">
        <p>On the recommended route, stop around <strong>89 Firemaking</strong>. If you deliberately chose the optional <strong>99 Firemaking</strong> route, stay until 99 instead. Search enough of the Reward Cart to give yourself working cash and supplies, then leave the rest banked in the cart if you prefer. Keep your <strong>gems, ores, herbs, seeds, raw fish and useful logs</strong>.</p>
        <p>Watch your <strong>magic logs</strong>: Chapter 1 eventually wants at least <strong>16</strong>. If Wintertodt does not give you enough, that is fine; 1.4 explicitly handles the shortage.</p>
        <div class="callout"><strong>1.1 complete:</strong> the account now has the early quest/travel foundation, a house, a useful bank and enough Firemaking that the skill can mostly disappear from the rest of Chapter 1.</div>
      </RouteStep>

    </article>
    <aside class="sidebar right-rail"><ProgressTracker storageKey="rsag-intermediate-1-1" title="1.1 progress" /></aside>
    <IntermediatePager nextHref={`${base}intermediate/chapter-1-2/`} nextLabel="Continue to 1.2 →" />
